8. DSA Concepts Used
This section includes concepts supported by the PRD and distinguishes them from possible implementations that need code verification.
DSA concept	Application in NetraGati
Strings	Representing and comparing vehicle registration numbers
String matching	Searching for a vehicle by its plate number
Edit distance	Fuzzy matching of OCR-misread characters
Searching	Retrieving matching vehicle sightings and watchlist entries
Sorting	Displaying sightings chronologically by timestamp
Arrays and lists	Processing collections of sightings, camera records and analytics results
Aggregation	Counting sightings and summarizing traffic by zone or hour
Important DSA details
String matching and edit distance: Your PRD explicitly identifies fuzzy/edit-distance search. The standard dynamic-programming edit-distance algorithm has \(O(mn)\) time complexity, where \(m\) and \(n\) are the lengths of the compared strings.
Sorting: Chronological ordering is part of the trajectory feature. A typical comparison-based sorting algorithm has \(O(n\log n)\) time complexity, although the exact algorithm used by your implementation should be verified.
Searching and aggregation: The system searches stored sightings and calculates traffic statistics. Actual performance depends on the database queries, indexes, and aggregation implementation.
Concepts not confirmed by the PRD: Linked lists, stacks, queues, trees, BFS, DFS, Dijkstra's algorithm, and custom hash tables are not explicitly identified as implemented features. Do not list them as DSA concepts actually used unless you confirm them in your source code.

